<section class="live-article-section">
<h2>The 4 C's of Diamonds</h2>
<p>When it comes to evaluating a diamond's quality, the 4 C's — Cut, Colour, Clarity, and Carat Weight — are essential factors to consider.</p>
</section>
<section class="live-article-section">
<h2>Colour</h2>
<p>Diamonds come in various shades, ranging from colourless to fancy vivid colours. The less colour a diamond has, the higher its value. The GIA colour grading scale begins with D, representing colourless, and progresses through to Z, representing diamonds with noticeable colour.</p>
<ol><li><strong>Colourless (D–F):</strong> highly valued for rarity and icy white appearance.</li><li><strong>Near Colourless (G–J):</strong> slight traces of colour, usually difficult to detect when mounted.</li><li><strong>Faint Yellow (K–M):</strong> a faint hue that becomes more noticeable down the scale.</li><li><strong>Very Light Yellow (N–R):</strong> noticeable yellow or brown tint.</li><li><strong>Light Yellow (S–Z):</strong> more pronounced colouration that can affect appearance and value.</li></ol>
<img class="article-image" alt="Diamond colour grading chart" src="{{ 'diamond-colour-grades-chart.webp' | asset_url }}">
</section>
<section class="live-article-section article-callout">
<h2>Carat Weight</h2>
<p>Carat weight is a measure of a diamond's size, with larger diamonds typically being more valuable. Price can change significantly at popular benchmark weights such as 0.50, 1.00 and 2.00 carats.</p>
<p>When choosing a diamond, balance carat weight with cut, colour and clarity to find the stone that best suits your preferences and budget.</p>
<div class="live-image-row"><img class="article-image" alt="Diamond clarity chart" src="{{ 'cameron-co-diamond-brilliant-cut-illustration.webp' | asset_url }}"><img class="article-image" alt="Diamond sizes" src="{{ 'diamond-shapes-chart.webp' | asset_url }}"></div>
</section>
<section class="live-article-section">
<h2>Cut</h2>
<p>Diamond cut refers to the way a diamond is shaped and faceted to enhance brilliance, fire, and overall appearance. A well-cut diamond can maximise its optical properties, whereas a poorly cut diamond may appear dull.</p>
<ul><li><strong>Proportions:</strong> depth, table size, crown angle and pavilion angle affect how light travels within the stone.</li><li><strong>Symmetry:</strong> balanced facets help distribute light throughout the diamond.</li><li><strong>Polish:</strong> surface quality can affect clarity and brilliance.</li><li><strong>Cut Grade:</strong> gemmological laboratories assign grades from Excellent to Poor.</li></ul>
<img class="article-image" alt="Anatomy of a diamond" src="{{ 'anatomy-of-a-diamond-chart.webp' | asset_url }}">
</section>
<section class="live-article-section">
<h2>Clarity</h2>
<p>Clarity refers to the presence of internal inclusions and external blemishes. The fewer inclusions, the higher the clarity grade. The GIA clarity scale ranges from Flawless to Included.</p>
<ol><li><strong>Flawless (FL):</strong> no inclusions or blemishes visible under 10x magnification.</li><li><strong>Internally Flawless (IF):</strong> no inclusions visible under 10x magnification.</li><li><strong>Very, Very Slightly Included (VVS1 and VVS2):</strong> inclusions are extremely difficult to see.</li><li><strong>Very Slightly Included (VS1 and VS2):</strong> minor inclusions visible under magnification but usually not to the naked eye.</li><li><strong>Slightly Included (SI1 and SI2):</strong> inclusions noticeable under magnification and sometimes visible.</li><li><strong>Included (I1, I2, I3):</strong> inclusions can affect transparency and brilliance.</li></ol>
